DT_COMPAT_ZMK_BEHAVIOR_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER := zmk,behavior-split-conn-ladder

menuconfig ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER
	bool "Step SPLIT-link connection parameters down while idle"
	depends on ZMK_SPLIT_BLE && ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
	help
	  For the CENTRAL half. Steps the split link's connection parameters
	  down while the keyboard is idle and restores them at the next key,
	  and with it, on this half: a low-power search for a missing other
	  half (LOW_POWER_PERIPHERAL_SEARCH) and an early deep sleep when
	  there is nothing to work for (EARLY_SLEEP).

	  Names in these help texts leave out the ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_
	  prefix: STEP1_LATENCY is ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_STEP1_LATENCY.

	  On the split link this half is central, so a parameter update is in
	  effect an order, applied at a Link Layer instant it picks: the
	  peripheral's host could object only through a le_param_req
	  callback, and ZMK registers none. The link to the computer is left
	  alone -- there this half is peripheral and could only ask.

	  The steps are computed by default (COMPUTED_STEPS), from a few
	  budgets and the link to the computer; with it off they are the table
	  STEP*_INTERVAL, STEP*_LATENCY and STEP*_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT.

	  Nothing outside this menu needs setting on this half. Steps are
	  applied from a work queue of the module's own, sized automatically
	  (WORK_QUEUE_STACK_SIZE). The other half needs no line either: its
	  report and its early sleep are on by default there.

	  Supports one split peripheral (ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_PERIPHERALS=1,
	  ZMK's default); the build stops otherwise.

if ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_BOOT_IN_AUTO_MODE
	bool "Boot in AUTO mode (the power source picks the steps)"
	default y
	help
	  The mode the keyboard boots into:

	    y  AUTO -- the power source decides (default):
	              on battery    the battery steps
	              on USB power  the USB power steps, see USB_POWER_STEPS;
	                            ACTIVE when that is off, or when the other
	                            half reports USB power too
	    n  ON   -- the battery steps whatever the power source

	  "USB power" on this half is anything that makes ZMK itself refuse deep
	  sleep: a computer, awake or asleep, or a bare charger.

	  The &ladder behavior (dts/behaviors/split_conn_ladder.dtsi) switches
	  between AUTO, ON and OFF at run time. That choice lives in RAM only:
	  every boot starts from this setting again.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_USB_POWER_STEPS
	bool "AUTO on USB power: USB power steps instead of holding ACTIVE"
	default y
	help
	  On USB power this half has nothing to save, but the other half is
	  still on its battery, and only latency saves it anything. So in AUTO
	  mode with USB power the same steps, on the same timers, switch to a
	  second parameter set, the USB power steps (STEP*_USB_POWER_*):
	  ACTIVE's interval with a higher latency. Keys from the other half
	  are not delayed by that: a peripheral with something to send
	  transmits at the very next connection event whatever its latency.
	  What waits is traffic TO it (the LED/indicator sync), up to
	  interval * (latency + 1).

	  Plugging in keeps the current step and swaps its parameters.
	  Unplugging returns to ACTIVE and restarts the idle clock.

	  When the other half reports USB power as well, there is nothing to
	  save anywhere and the ladder holds ACTIVE. The report is on by
	  default on the other half (PERIPHERAL_USB_POWER_REPORT); a half
	  built without it counts as on battery.

	  n: AUTO on USB power holds ACTIVE.

config ZMK_BEHAVIOR_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER
	bool
	default $(dt_compat_enabled,$(DT_COMPAT_ZMK_BEHAVIOR_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER))
	help
	  Turns itself on when the keymap references &ladder. Nothing to set
	  -- and do not: it has no prompt, and Zephyr stops the build when a
	  .conf assigns a symbol without one.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_WORK_QUEUE_STACK_SIZE
	int "Stack of the ladder's work queue (bytes)"
	default 2048 if LOG
	default 1280
	help
	  Sized for the deepest things this queue runs, measured on real
	  builds: applying a step, which ends in a blocking HCI command, about
	  800 bytes; with logging (every LOG_* call packs its arguments on the
	  stack, and the Bluetooth host logs from inside that command) printing
	  the computed table, about 1340, and up to 1556 should a connection be
	  released inside that walk. An interrupt adds up to 104 bytes on top.
	  The low-power search's scan start and stop, and the other half's
	  report, run here too and need less. The defaults leave about 340
	  to 390 bytes spare; there is no reason to set this. The build
	  stops if it is set below them.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_WORK_QUEUE_PRIORITY
	int "Priority of the ladder's work queue thread"
	default 10
	help
	  Preemptible and low, the same as ZMK's own low-priority queue: a
	  step change is never urgent against key processing.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_STEP_COUNT
	int "Number of idle steps"
	range 1 6
	default 4
	help
	  How many steps are built, up to six. Steps numbered above this
	  count are ignored entirely -- not built, not checked -- so leaving
	  their values at the defaults is harmless.

	  Four steps on the default timers begin 0, 3, 10 and 20 minutes into
	  idle. The deepest, 398.75 ms at latency 0, costs the central half
	  about 60 uA and the other half about 30 uA, and comes back in about
	  3 s, where waking from sleep takes a reboot and a reconnect: README,
	  "Step 4 instead of sleep".

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_MIN_SUPERVISION_WINDOWS
	int "Minimum supervision windows demanded of every step"
	range 2 40
	default 6
	help
	  How many peripheral attendance periods the supervision timeout must
	  cover, checked for every step and for ACTIVE. This is how many of
	  the peripheral's scheduled wake-ups may be missed before the link is
	  declared dead.

	  Bluetooth itself permits as low as 2, which is not survivable on a
	  board running the nRF52840 internal RC oscillator. It also stops a
	  deep step from quietly becoming more fragile than a shallow one.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_MAX_RETURN_MS
	int "Longest permitted return from any step (ms)"
	range 500 20000
	default 4000
	help
	  Leaving a step costs interval * (3 * latency + 8), and for that whole
	  time every keystroke from the peripheral half is still delayed by a
	  full interval. This is the hard ceiling for every step, table or
	  computed, USB power ones included; the computed steps have tighter
	  budgets of their own (RETURN_BUDGET_MS,
	  DEEPEST_STEP_RETURN_BUDGET_MS).

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_COMPUTED_STEPS
	bool "Compute the steps (off: take them from the table)"
	default y
	help
	  On (the default), the ladder works the parameters out itself, and
	  works them out again whenever the link to the computer, the output
	  or the other half's power changes:

	    ACTIVE   ACTIVE_DEFAULT_INTERVAL (16.25 ms) -- or, with a pointer
	             whose motion goes to the computer over Bluetooth, an
	             interval that keeps out of the host link's way: below it,
	             sharing a factor and not dividing it, not below 12.5 ms
	             (12.5 ms against a Mac's 15 ms), otherwise above it with
	             no common factor (16.25 ms against a PC's 7.5 ms). With
	             the output on USB, 8.75 ms. The latency makes what the
	             other half is sent wait about
	             ACTIVE_PERIPHERAL_SYNC_DELAY_MS, and is 0 when the other
	             half reports USB power.
	    step k   a latency from a fixed series (STEP<k>_LATENCY sets it),
	             and the longest interval that still returns to ACTIVE
	             within the step's budget -- RETURN_BUDGET_MS, or
	             DEEPEST_STEP_RETURN_BUDGET_MS for the deepest -- keeps
	             MIN_SUPERVISION_WINDOWS attendances inside the timeout,
	             and shares no factor with 2, 3 or the host link's
	             interval.
	    step k   ACTIVE's interval, which delays no key; the latency rises
	    on USB   in equal steps from ACTIVE's to the one that makes the
	    power    other half's sync delay USB_POWER_PERIPHERAL_SYNC_DELAY_MS.

	  With the defaults and four steps: 31.25 ms at latency 13, 83.75 ms
	  at 3, 136.25 ms at 1, 398.75 ms at 0 -- returns of 1.47, 1.42, 1.50
	  and 3.19 s.

	  Any step can be set by hand: STEP*_LATENCY, STEP*_INTERVAL,
	  STEP*_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT and, for USB power,
	  STEP*_USB_POWER_LATENCY and STEP*_USB_POWER_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT
	  default to -1 or 0, "computed", in this mode -- see STEP1_INTERVAL.
	  The log prints the whole table at every recompute ("steps computed
	  ...").

	  Off, the steps are the table -- STEP*_INTERVAL, STEP*_LATENCY,
	  STEP*_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT and STEP*_USB_POWER_* -- and ACTIVE is
	  ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_PREF_INT/_LATENCY/_TIMEOUT.

if ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_COMPUTED_STEPS

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_ACTIVE_AVOIDS_HOST_INTERVAL
	bool "A trackball or other pointer sends through this keyboard"
	default y if ZMK_POINTING
	help
	  Whether ACTIVE is chosen against the link to the computer. Motion
	  reaches the computer over this half's host link whichever half the
	  sensor is on, and a split interval whose events keep landing on
	  that link's shows up as a cursor that stutters. Without a pointer
	  the collisions only cost a key a few milliseconds now and then, and
	  ACTIVE stays ACTIVE_DEFAULT_INTERVAL.

	  n is also the way to keep ACTIVE at ACTIVE_DEFAULT_INTERVAL on a
	  Mac, where the choice against its 15 ms link is 12.5 ms.

	  Follows ZMK_POINTING, which a trackball half turns on.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_ACTIVE_DEFAULT_INTERVAL
	int "ACTIVE interval without a host link to avoid (1.25 ms units)"
	range 6 16
	default 13
	help
	  The split interval while typing, when no host link is there to keep
	  out of the way of -- no pointer, no Bluetooth host, nothing that
	  fits (see COMPUTED_STEPS). 13 is 16.25 ms: shares no factor with a
	  PC's 7.5 ms or a Mac's 15 ms link. With the output on USB ACTIVE is
	  8.75 ms whatever this says.

	  16 (20 ms) at most: the steps must stay longer than any ACTIVE.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_PERIPHERAL_SHOWS_INDICATORS
	bool "The other half shows what this half sends it (LED, layer colour)"
	default y if RGBLED_WIDGET_LAYER_PUSH || ZMK_SPLIT_PERIPHERAL_HID_INDICATORS
	help
	  Sets the default of ACTIVE_PERIPHERAL_SYNC_DELAY_MS: about 160 ms
	  while typing when the other half has something to show -- a layer
	  colour pushed by zmk-rgbled-widget, the host's caps-lock -- and
	  400 ms when it has not. On by itself with RGBLED_WIDGET_LAYER_PUSH or
	  ZMK_SPLIT_PERIPHERAL_HID_INDICATORS.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_ACTIVE_PERIPHERAL_SYNC_DELAY_MS
	int "ACTIVE: how long what the other half is sent may wait (ms)"
	range 0 5000
	default 163 if ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_PERIPHERAL_SHOWS_INDICATORS
	default 400
	help
	  ACTIVE's latency is picked so that the other half's attendance,
	  interval * (latency + 1), comes nearest this: the delay of an LED
	  or layer push while typing. Keys from the other half are not
	  delayed by latency. 163 gives 162.5 ms on the 16.25 ms and 12.5 ms
	  intervals; 400 gives 406, 400 and 400 ms on 16.25, 12.5 and 20 ms.
	  When the other half reports USB power the latency is 0.

	  Must not exceed USB_POWER_PERIPHERAL_SYNC_DELAY_MS, where the USB
	  power steps end.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_RETURN_BUDGET_MS
	int "Return budget from every step but the deepest (ms)"
	range 500 20000
	default 1500
	help
	  How long coming back from a step to ACTIVE may take. For that whole
	  time the other half's keys still wait up to a full step interval.
	  Every step gets the longest interval that returns within it at the
	  step's latency, so a deeper step -- a lower latency -- gets a longer
	  interval and saves more, and is no slower to leave.

	  Below about 1000 ms the series' first latency, 13, leaves step 1
	  nothing longer than 20 ms, and the build stops asking for a lower
	  STEP1_LATENCY.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_DEEPEST_STEP_RETURN_BUDGET_MS
	int "Return budget from the deepest step (ms, 0: RETURN_BUDGET_MS)"
	range 0 20000
	default 3200
	help
	  The deepest step may take longer to leave: 3200 lets it reach
	  398.75 ms at latency 0, the step that stands in for sleep. 0 gives
	  it RETURN_BUDGET_MS like the others. Must not be below
	  RETURN_BUDGET_MS nor above MAX_RETURN_MS.

	  It also picks the latency series: with a deepest budget of its own
	  the deepest step is latency 0 and the others take the series of one
	  step fewer (four steps: 13, 3, 1, then 0), with 0 all steps share
	  one series (four steps: 13, 3, 1, 0) -- STEP1_LATENCY lists them.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_STEPS_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT
	int "Supervision timeout of every computed step (10 ms units)"
	range 100 3200
	default 800
	help
	  With MIN_SUPERVISION_WINDOWS this caps each step's interval at its
	  latency: the timeout must cover that many attendances. A step's own
	  STEP*_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT replaces it for that step. ACTIVE has its
	  own, ACTIVE_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_ACTIVE_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT
	int "Supervision timeout of ACTIVE (10 ms units)"
	range 100 3200
	default 400
	help
	  How long the halves go without hearing each other at ACTIVE before
	  the link is declared lost. ACTIVE's attendance is short -- 163 ms
	  with PERIPHERAL_SHOWS_INDICATORS, about 400 ms without -- so 4 s,
	  ZMK's own default, still covers about ten to twenty-four of them. Shorter
	  than the steps' on purpose: until the link is declared lost this
	  half does not know the other one has gone, and anything it sends
	  that way meanwhile waits in the Bluetooth buffers it shares with the
	  reports to the computer. The latency is lowered, if need be, to keep
	  MIN_SUPERVISION_WINDOWS in it.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_USB_POWER_PERIPHERAL_SYNC_DELAY_MS
	int "On USB power: longest delay of what the other half is sent (ms)"
	depends on ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_USB_POWER_STEPS
	range 0 10000
	default 625
	help
	  The USB power steps keep ACTIVE's interval, so a key from the other
	  half is never delayed; they raise only the latency, and all that
	  costs is how long something sent TO the other half waits -- the
	  LED/layer push -- which is interval * (latency + 1). The latency
	  rises in equal steps from ACTIVE's to the one that makes this delay,
	  reached at the deepest step: on 16.25 ms and four steps, 16, 23, 30,
	  37 -- 276, 390, 504 and 617.5 ms -- with PERIPHERAL_SHOWS_INDICATORS
	  (ACTIVE at latency 9); without it, from latency 24: 27, 30, 33, 37. Leaving a USB power step delays no
	  key either, so the return budgets do not apply to them;
	  MIN_SUPERVISION_WINDOWS and MAX_RETURN_MS do. 0 keeps every USB
	  power step at ACTIVE.

endif # ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_COMPUTED_STEPS

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_NEW_LINK_SETTLE_MS
	int "Delay before re-applying a step to a freshly connected link (ms)"
	range 0 30000
	default 3000
	help
	  How long the ladder keeps its hands off a split link that has just
	  connected. In those first seconds ZMK sets the link up -- encryption,
	  GATT discovery, subscriptions -- and a parameter update would only
	  queue behind that.

	  No step is applied in that window, whatever wakes the ladder -- a
	  key, a cable, the &ladder key, the other half's power report. That
	  is what protects the link with STEP1_AFTER_IDLE_MS at 0, the
	  default.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_REJECTED_UPDATE_RETRY_MS
	int "Retry delay after a rejected parameter update (ms)"
	range 50 5000
	default 500
	help
	  First retry after a refused update, logged as "param update
	  rejected: N -- retry K in M ms". Zephyr 3.5's controller queues Link
	  Layer procedures rather than refusing a second one, so a refusal is
	  rare and short-lived: -105 (ENOBUFS, no free HCI command buffer) or
	  -5 (EIO, the controller had no free procedure context). Each further
	  refusal in a row doubles the wait, up to
	  UNCONFIRMED_UPDATE_TIMEOUT_MS; the first
	  accepted update resets it. Restoring ACTIVE retries on the same
	  schedule.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_UNCONFIRMED_UPDATE_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "How long an unconfirmed parameter update blocks a deeper step (ms)"
	range 1000 60000
	default 15000
	help
	  A descent waits for the previous update to be confirmed, so the
	  peripheral never has to process two Link Layer instants back to
	  back. If the confirmation is lost, this is when we stop waiting.
	  Restoring ACTIVE never waits -- coming back up is urgent, and the
	  controller queues that procedure rather than refusing it. Must exceed
	  MAX_RETURN_MS, the longest a step may take to leave; the build stops
	  otherwise.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_BACKOFF_AFTER_LINK_LOSS
	bool "Reduce ladder depth after a split link loss"
	default y
	help
	  A dropped link is evidence that the depth it dropped at does not
	  hold on this hardware, in this room. Rather than retrying the same
	  depth forever, cap the ladder one step shallower and hand the notch
	  back once the link has proven stable. Neighbouring steps with the
	  same parameters count as one step here, and the battery and the
	  USB power steps are capped separately.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_BACKOFF_RESTORE_AFTER_MS
	int "Continuous connection time before one depth notch is restored (ms)"
	depends on ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_BACKOFF_AFTER_LINK_LOSS
	range 60000 21600000
	default 900000
	help
	  Doubled on each further failure, up to 32x and never beyond a week,
	  so a depth that keeps failing stops being retried within a few hours
	  instead of costing a dropped link every time this elapses.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_BACKOFF_RECONNECT_WINDOW_MS
	int "Reconnect window that confirms a loss was a step failure (ms)"
	depends on ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_BACKOFF_AFTER_LINK_LOSS
	range 5000 600000
	default 60000
	help
	  A disconnect alone does not prove the step was too deep: the other
	  half also drops the link when it goes to sleep. With
	  PERIPHERAL_CLOSE_LINK_BEFORE_SLEEP there it closes the link first,
	  and a closed link is never judged. Without it -- and on a reset or
	  a pulled battery -- the link just stops, which from here looks
	  exactly like a supervision timeout. A half that lost the link over
	  parameters is awake and advertising, so it returns within seconds;
	  a half that powered itself off stays away until a key is pressed on
	  it. A loss is only counted against the ladder if the link comes
	  back inside this window -- and, with the other half's report, only
	  if that half did not restart meanwhile, whatever the timing.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_LOG_RSSI
	bool "Log split link RSSI on every step change"
	default n
	help
	  Answers the question connection parameters cannot: when a step
	  fails, is the signal weak or is the band merely busy? A split link
	  across a desk should sit around -40..-65 dBm; if it does and steps
	  still fail, the losses are congestion, and channel classification is
	  the lever rather than anything here.

	  Needs CONFIG_BT_CTLR_CONN_RSSI=y, which ZMK leaves off, or the
	  controller answers "unknown command". Diagnostic only.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_LOW_POWER_PERIPHERAL_SEARCH
	bool "Low-power search for the other half once it has been gone a while"
	default y
	help
	  ZMK searches for a missing other half with half the radio's time
	  -- a 30 ms scan window every 60 ms, about 2.5 mA -- for as long as
	  it is missing: all night, when that half is asleep or switched off.

	  With this on, ZMK searches like that -- or the module's middle
	  search instead, below -- for ABSENCE_WAIT_MS after the other half
	  disappears (or after boot); then the module switches ZMK's search
	  off and listens itself for 30 ms every 1.28 s, about 0.1 mA. When it hears the other half -- a waking half calls for
	  1.28 s at full rate, so one window catches it -- ZMK's full search
	  comes back and connects. What wakes it:

	    - directed advertising, which a half sends only to the central it
	      is paired with -- the radio drops any addressed elsewhere;
	    - a ZMK split half advertising for pairing, if it is the half
	      this one is paired with (a half that lost its pairing keeps its
	      address), or if this half has no paired half yet.

	  A call that does not end in a connection counts as a loss at that
	  moment: ABSENCE_WAIT_MS more, then the low-power search again.

	  The full search stays on only while the keys go to a USB host:
	  there it costs no battery, and no Bluetooth link to a computer
	  carries the output for it to take airtime from. USB power alone
	  changes nothing: on a charger, or on a computer's cable with the
	  keys on Bluetooth, the search goes as on battery.

	  While the keys go to a Bluetooth host, the first ABSENCE_WAIT_MS
	  are not ZMK's full search but a middle one of the module's own
	  (MIDDLE_PERIPHERAL_SEARCH_INTERVAL_MS): the full search takes half
	  the radio, and the host link loses events to it -- a trackball
	  stutters.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_MIDDLE_PERIPHERAL_SEARCH_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Middle search: listen every this many ms while the keys go to a Bluetooth host (0: off)"
	depends on ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_LOW_POWER_PERIPHERAL_SEARCH
	range 0 1280
	default 240
	help
	  The middle search: for the first ABSENCE_WAIT_MS after the other
	  half goes, while the keys go to a connected Bluetooth host, the
	  module listens 30 ms every this many ms instead of ZMK's full
	  search (30 ms every 60 ms). At 240 ms that is an eighth of the
	  radio's time instead of half: the host link loses far fewer events,
	  and the search draws about 0.6 mA instead of 2.5 mA.

	  It cannot miss the other half, only hear it a little later: a half
	  that has just woken calls at full rate for 1.28 s, which the first
	  window catches -- within this interval; after that it calls every
	  100-150 ms, which a 30 ms window catches about one time in four --
	  within about four intervals on average. Hearing it, the module
	  switches ZMK's full search on for 2 s, which connects.

	  With the keys on USB, or with no Bluetooth host connected, ZMK's
	  full search runs instead: there it takes nothing from a host link.
	  After ABSENCE_WAIT_MS the low-power search follows either way.

	  0 turns the middle search off: ZMK's full search for the first
	  ABSENCE_WAIT_MS in every case. Other values below 60 stop the build
	  -- 60 ms is the full search itself.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_EARLY_SLEEP_WITHOUT_PERIPHERAL
	bool "Sleep early without the other half, even with a computer"
	depends on ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_EARLY_SLEEP && ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
	default n
	help
	  For a keyboard that is no use without its other half. With this on,
	  this half also goes to sleep after ABSENCE_WAIT_MS of idle without
	  the other half -- while a computer is connected too -- unless it is on
	  USB power with a computer there. A key or the trackball wakes it.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_EARLY_SLEEP_BLE_PAIRING_WAIT_MS
	int "Early sleep while the active BLE profile waits for a computer to pair (ms)"
	depends on ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_EARLY_SLEEP && ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
	range 10000 3600000
	default 180000
	help
	  While the active Bluetooth profile has no computer paired to it,
	  the keyboard is waiting for one to pair, and the early sleep waits
	  this long instead of ABSENCE_WAIT_MS, whatever the reason to sleep.
	  A computer that connects to pair starts the count again.
	  Must not be below ABSENCE_WAIT_MS nor below the settings-save
	  formula (see ABSENCE_WAIT_MS).

#
# Units are Bluetooth units, not milliseconds:
#   interval -- 1.25 ms steps:  13 = 16.25 ms   25 = 31.25 ms   67 = 83.75 ms
#                               109 = 136.25 ms 319 = 398.75 ms
#   timeout  -- 10 ms steps:   400 = 4 s    800 = 8 s
#   latency  -- a plain count of connection events
#
# Derived quantities, all in milliseconds:
#   LED sync delay, and how often the peripheral must be on air
#                                   = interval * (latency + 1) * 1.25
#   leaving a step (any direction)  = interval * (3 * latency + 8) * 1.25
#                                     -- of the step being LEFT
#   first key from the other half after a step = interval * 1.25 (worst case)
#
# Hard requirement, checked at build time and by Zephyr at runtime:
#   timeout * 4 > (1 + latency) * interval
#
# Every step has two parameter sets on one timer (STEP*_AFTER_IDLE_MS): the
# battery set, STEP*_INTERVAL, STEP*_LATENCY and STEP*_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT,
# and the USB power set, STEP*_USB_POWER_*, used in AUTO mode on USB power
# (USB_POWER_STEPS). Names leave out the ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_ prefix.
#
# With COMPUTED_STEPS (the default) the parameter symbols default to 0
# (interval, timeout) and -1 (latency) -- "computed" -- and a value set here
# sets that one thing for that step: see STEP1_INTERVAL, STEP1_LATENCY.
# Without it they are the table. Its battery steps default to what the
# computation gives with no computer in the way: 25/13, 67/3, 109/1 and
# 319/0, steps 5 and 6 repeating step 4. Its USB power steps run
# ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_PREF_INT at latency 29 (steps 1, 2) and 49 (steps 3 to 6),
# which suits PREF 10/12/600; with ZMK's own PREF_LATENCY of 30, steps 1 and
# 2 are shallower than ACTIVE (README, "The table").
#
# The step intervals are deliberately not round numbers: 25, 67, 109 and 319
# share no factor with the 12 units (15 ms) a Mac gives its own link, nor
# with the 6 (7.5 ms) a PC does. A central half with a link to a computer of
# its own runs both links on one radio, and an interval that is a multiple of
# the computer's parks the two schedules on top of each other for as long as
# the clocks take to drift apart -- README, "The interval and the computer's
# own link".
#

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_STEP1_AFTER_IDLE_MS
	int "Step 1: delay after entering idle (ms)"
	range 0 86400000
	default 0
	help
	  How long after entering idle the step begins. 0, the default,
	  begins at once: ZMK_IDLE_TIMEOUT (30 s without a key) is the
	  hysteresis, and short pauses in typing cost no parameter update.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_STEP1_INTERVAL
	int "Step 1: connection interval (1.25 ms units)"
	range 0 3200 if ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_COMPUTED_STEPS
	range 6 3200
	default 0 if ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_COMPUTED_STEPS
	default 25
	help
	  Computed mode (COMPUTED_STEPS): 0, the default there, computes the
	  interval -- the longest that returns within the step's budget at its
	  latency and keeps MIN_SUPERVISION_WINDOWS inside its timeout, on a
	  number that shares no factor with 2, 3 or the host link's interval.
	  Any other value sets it: the step runs exactly this interval, at
	  STEP1_LATENCY or the series latency, and the build checks it --
	  within the step's budget, MIN_SUPERVISION_WINDOWS and the Bluetooth
	  rule, at least 17
	  (20 ms is the longest ACTIVE), and not shallower than the step above
	  (a step that repeats the one above only draws a warning).

	  Table mode: the step's interval.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_STEP1_LATENCY
	int "Step 1: peripheral latency (events)"
	range -1 499 if ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_COMPUTED_STEPS
	range 0 499
	default -1 if ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_COMPUTED_STEPS
	default 13
	help
	  Computed mode: -1, the default there, takes the series latency for
	  this step, by the number of steps and whether the deepest has a
	  budget of its own (DEEPEST_STEP_RETURN_BUDGET_MS):

	               a budget of its own    DEEPEST_STEP_RETURN_BUDGET_MS=0
	    1 step     0                      13
	    2 steps    13, 0                  13, 3
	    3 steps    13, 3, 0               13, 3, 1
	    4 steps    13, 3, 1, 0            13, 3, 1, 0
	    5 steps    13, 3, 1, 0, 0         13, 5, 3, 1, 0
	    6 steps    13, 5, 3, 1, 0, 0      13, 7, 5, 3, 1, 0

	  A value sets the latency, and the interval is computed for it
	  (unless STEP1_INTERVAL sets that too). The build stops if nothing
	  longer than 20 ms returns within the step's budget at this latency:
	  at most 20 with a 1500 ms budget, 47 with 3200 ms.

	  Table mode: the step's latency.

config ZMK_SPLIT_CONN_LADDER_STEP1_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT
